+### Executor compatibility
+
+`GET /health` and `/v1/health` advertise `features: ["idempotent_submit", "job_lookup", "cancel"]`. `POST /v1/jobs` accepts an optional `idempotency_key` of exactly 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters. A replay returns HTTP 202 with the original job's current metadata, even after device configuration changes; a different submitted body with the same key returns HTTP 409. `GET /v1/jobs/by-key/<key>` returns that metadata or HTTP 404 across restarts, independently of the recent 100-job listing. Original submitted fields, canonical `submission_hash`, candidate ID, and frozen device selection remain stable.
+
+`POST /v1/jobs/<id>/cancel` returns HTTP 200 metadata: queued jobs become `cancelled` immediately; running jobs persist `cancel_requested` and finish the existing backend experiment and cleanup. Cancellation can therefore take the entire remaining experiment duration; there is no bounded acknowledgement latency or process/phone kill. Terminal `cancelled` requires `cleanup_status: "completed"`. Result files and logs remain available.
+
+Cleanup checks the actual `conditions_observed.restored`, optional `recovery.status == "reported_complete"`, GPU-lock restoration, and foreground receipts. A safely restored ABORT remains executor `completed`. Unknown or unsafe cleanup becomes `uncertain`; interrupted jobs also retain uncertain cleanup. Both hold all further phone jobs across restart (`health.status: "blocked"`, `blocked_jobs`) until explicit operator reconciliation. Queued cancellation and clean queued recovery remain available. This confirms owned-resource cleanup, not restoration of game state.
+
+After a local operator has independently confirmed the old process stopped and the device was restored, `POST /v1/jobs/<id>/resolve` with `{"confirmed_stopped":true,"confirmed_restored":true}` releases an inactive uncertain/interrupted job as cancelled. It records `cleanup_confirmation.source="operator"`, preserves original reports, and never pretends the backend verified those facts. Running jobs and incomplete/false confirmations are rejected. The SDK job must then be resolved separately using its normal workflow; neither layer automatically repeats the old experiment.
+
+Shader jobs freeze only source/SPIR-V, the implicit sibling `baseline.spv`, and reference images into read-only job-local files before queue admission. Directory references use the same evenly sampled maximum of 32 frames as upstream. SHA256 manifests are checked before device execution; original submitted-body hashes exclude the optional key and precede path rewriting. Other job kinds retain their existing inputs.
+
